ground_truth:
We expand each product separately: \begin{align*}
(2x-5)(x+7) &= 2x(x) + 2x(7) -5(x) -5(7)\\
&=2x^2 +14x - 5x -35\\
&= 2x^2 +9x - 35
\end{align*}and \begin{align*}
(x+5)(2x-1) &=x(2x) + x(-1) +5(2x) + 5(-1)\\
&=2x^2 -x + 10x -5\\
&=2x^2 +9x - 5.
\end{align*}So, we have  \begin{align*}&\ \ \ \ (2x-5)(x+7) - (x+5)(2x-1) \\&= 2x^2+9x -35 - (2x^2 +9x -5) = \boxed{-30}.\end{align*}

question:
Compute the ordered pair of positive integers $(x,y)$ such that

\begin{align*}
x^y+1&=y^x,\\
2x^y&=y^x+7.
\end{align*}

ground_truth:
The greatest common factor of the two coefficients is $11$, and the greatest power of $z$ that divides both terms is $z^{17}$. So, we factor $11z^{17}$ out of both terms:

\begin{align*}
55z^{17}+121z^{34} &= 11z^{17}\cdot 5 +11z^{17}\cdot 11z^{17}\\
&= \boxed{11z^{17}(5+11z^{17})}
\end{align*}

